-- Passage rg5-character-setting-plot-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('207330a4-d5a4-562c-bb25-5f9a7bf4fc05', 'rg5-character-setting-plot-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '3-5', $q$The Slate in the Box$q$, $q$Mira Castellano's class was studying how schools have changed, so she asked her great-grandfather Wen about his first school. He opened a wooden box and pulled out a small black slate in a cracked frame.

"This was my notebook," he said. "We wrote with chalk and wiped it clean with a rag."

His school had been a single room on the prairie. Twenty students, ages six to fourteen, shared one teacher. In winter, the oldest boy arrived early to light the iron stove. The youngest children sat in the front rows, closest to the heat. Wen walked two miles each way, even in snow.

Mira thought about her own school. It had three floors, a gym and a library with forty computers. She rode a yellow bus that stopped right outside her apartment building. Her class had twenty-six students, all ten or eleven years old, and there were special teachers for music and art.

"Didn't you get bored, waiting while the teacher helped the big kids?" Mira asked.

Wen smiled. "Never. I listened to their lessons, too. By the time I was eight, I knew my times tables up to twelve."

Mira thought about that. In her class, everyone learned the same lesson at the same time. In Wen's school, a little kid could listen in and learn from older students' lessons. She decided to write her report about that difference.$q$, 'draft')
