-- Passage rg5-connections-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('115f6848-b8f4-5609-b93e-5609a25cedc7', 'rg5-connections-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-connections'), null,
        '3-5', $q$How a Hot Air Balloon Rises$q$, $q$A hot air balloon has three main parts. The envelope is the large fabric bag. The basket hangs below it and carries the riders. Between them is the burner, which makes a strong flame.

The balloon flies because of a simple fact: warm air is lighter than cool air. When the pilot turns on the burner, the flame heats the air inside the envelope. The warm air spreads out, and some of it slips out of the bottom. Now the air inside is lighter than the same amount of cooler air outside. The balloon begins to rise.

To go higher, the pilot heats the air again. To come down, the pilot lets the air inside cool, or opens a vent at the top. The vent lets some warm air escape, and cooler air takes its place.

A pilot cannot steer a balloon the way a driver steers a car. Instead, the pilot uses the wind. Winds often blow in different directions at different heights. By rising or sinking, a pilot can find a wind that carries the balloon the right way.

That is why balloon pilots study the weather before every flight. Calm mornings are often best, because strong winds make landing difficult.$q$, 'draft')
