-- Passage rg5-connections-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b35465dd-cd35-57c0-be2a-8d5f11a3e46f', 'rg5-connections-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-connections'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Fig Trees and Their Tiny Partners$q$, $q$A fig looks like a fruit, but it is really a small pouch with hundreds of tiny flowers inside. Because the flowers are hidden, bees and butterflies cannot reach them. Fig trees need a different helper: a wasp smaller than a grain of rice.

Each kind of fig tree has its own kind of fig wasp. A female wasp squeezes into a young fig through a tiny hole at its tip. She carries pollen from the fig where she was born. Inside, she lays her eggs and spreads the pollen on the flowers. The pollinated flowers can then make seeds.

The wasp's young grow inside the fig. When they are grown, the new females collect pollen and leave through a tunnel. They fly off to find young figs of their own, and the cycle starts again.

Neither partner can survive without the other. The wasps need figs as a safe place to raise their young. The fig trees need wasps to carry pollen so they can make seeds.

This partnership matters to other animals too. In some tropical forests, different fig trees ripen at different times of year. When other fruits are scarce, monkeys, birds, and bats can still find ripe figs to eat. Scientists say that protecting fig wasps helps protect a whole forest community.$q$, 'draft')
