-- Passage rg5-evidence-inference-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('31eed681-81aa-5f68-b72d-83a9486dcc88', 'rg5-evidence-inference-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-evidence-inference'), null,
        '3-5', $q$A Long Trip for a Tiny Bird$q$, $q$The ruby-throated hummingbird weighs less than a nickel. Yet each fall, many of these tiny birds fly from the eastern part of North America to Central America. Some cross a wide stretch of sea without stopping. That trip is about five hundred miles over open water.

To get ready, a hummingbird eats and eats. In the weeks before it leaves, it may nearly double its weight. The extra weight is stored fat, which the bird burns like fuel during the long flight. A bird that sets out too thin may not have enough energy to finish.

Hummingbirds travel alone, not in flocks. Young birds make the trip for the first time without an adult to follow. Scientists are still studying how the young birds know which way to go. Many believe the birds are born with a sense of direction.

People can help hummingbirds along the way. Flowers such as bee balm and trumpet vine give them nectar. Some people hang feeders filled with sugar water. Experts suggest leaving feeders up for a few weeks after the last bird is seen. A late traveler passing through may still need a meal.$q$, 'draft')
