-- Passage rg5-evidence-inference-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('290c9b49-2cf4-5706-8df6-56df663afd45', 'rg5-evidence-inference-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-evidence-inference'), null,
        '3-5', $q$How a Suspension Bridge Hangs$q$, $q$A suspension bridge can stretch across a wide river or bay. The road seems to float in the air. It is really held up by a clever system of cables and towers.

First, builders put up two tall towers, one near each shore. Then they string two main cables over the tops of the towers. The ends of the main cables are fastened into huge blocks of concrete on land. These blocks are called anchorages, and they keep the cables from pulling loose.

Next, workers hang many thinner cables straight down from the main cables. These are called suspenders. The road deck is attached to the bottom of the suspenders. The weight of the road and the cars pulls down on the suspenders. The suspenders pass that pull to the main cables, and the main cables pass it to the towers and anchorages.

A main cable is not one thick rope of steel. It is made of thousands of thin steel wires bundled together. If a few wires wear out, the others still share the load. Crews check the cables often and replace worn parts.

Wind is a special challenge for long bridges. Engineers test small models in wind tunnels before building. Many newer bridges have a road deck shaped to let air flow smoothly around it.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
where public.passages.status = 'draft';
