-- Passage rg5-evidence-inference-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6593489f-ceb8-561a-8b3b-e3076c9e1598', 'rg5-evidence-inference-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-evidence-inference'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Basketball on Wheels$q$, $q$Wheelchair basketball is played on the same size court as regular basketball. The hoop is the same height, ten feet off the floor. Players shoot, pass and dribble, just like in any other game. The big difference is that every player moves in a sport wheelchair.

A sport wheelchair looks different from an everyday one. Its wheels tilt inward at the bottom, which makes the chair harder to tip over during quick turns. A small extra wheel sticks out the back to keep the chair from flipping backward. Many chairs also have a metal bumper at the front.

Some rules are changed to fit the chairs. A player may push the wheels twice while holding the ball. After that, the player must pass, shoot or bounce the ball. Taking a third push is a traveling foul.

The sport has grown over the years. Today, teams play in schools, colleges and community leagues. Some teams include players who can walk but have injuries to their knees or ankles.

Shooting from a seated position takes strong arms and shoulders. A player cannot jump to add power to a shot. Many players say the hardest skill to learn is pushing the chair and handling the ball at the same time.$q$, 'draft')
