-- Passage rg5-evidence-inference-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6a6d670c-33e5-5154-9a57-ce09c9a1e33e', 'rg5-evidence-inference-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-evidence-inference'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Paper Made by Hand$q$, $q$Long ago, paper was made one sheet at a time by hand. In many places, the main ingredient was old cloth. Papermakers collected worn shirts and sheets made of cotton or linen. They cut the cloth into small pieces and soaked it in water for days.

Next, the soaked cloth was beaten until the threads broke apart into tiny fibers. The fibers floated in a big tub of water. This made a thin, cloudy mixture called pulp.

To form a sheet, the papermaker dipped a screen set in a wooden frame into the tub. The papermaker lifted it out level and gave it a gentle shake. The water drained through the screen, and a thin layer of fibers stayed on top. Shaking the screen side to side helped the fibers lock together.

The wet sheet was flipped onto a piece of felt. Workers stacked sheets and felts into a tall pile. Then they squeezed out more water with a heavy press. Finally, the sheets were hung up to dry.

Because rags were the main ingredient, paper was often hard to get. Some towns asked families to save every scrap of cloth. Later, people learned to make paper from wood. Wood was far easier to find, and paper became much cheaper.$q$, 'draft')
