insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('fbb1ed0c-1000-55b4-aff4-3ce4455a0460', 'rg5-evidence-inference-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-evidence-inference'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Stories Told in Shadows$q$, $q$Shadow puppet theater is one of the oldest forms of storytelling. It has been performed in many parts of the world for hundreds of years. The idea is simple. A light shines on a screen, and puppets held behind the screen cast shadows the audience can see.

Traditional shadow puppets are flat. Many are cut from heavy paper or thin, dried animal hide. Puppet makers punch tiny holes in patterns across the figure. When light passes through the holes, the shadow shows details like the lace on a dress or the feathers on a bird. Some puppets are colored and partly see-through, so their shadows glow in soft shades of red, green and gold.

Each puppet is moved with thin rods. One rod holds up the body. Others are attached to the arms, so a puppet can wave, point or bow. A skilled performer may work two or three puppets at once.

The distance between the puppet and the light matters. When a puppet moves closer to the light, its shadow grows larger and blurrier. When it presses against the screen, the shadow becomes small and sharp. Performers use this trick to make a giant appear or a bird fly away into the distance.

Many shows include music and a narrator. In some traditions, one performer speaks every character's voice while also moving the puppets.$q$, 'draft')
