-- Passage rg5-figurative-language-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('03e0eb7d-ec8f-5b7a-bb86-45fc728e4fcf', 'rg5-figurative-language-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-figurative-language'), null,
        '3-5', $q$How a Thunderstorm Works$q$, $q$On a hot summer afternoon, the sky can change in a heartbeat. Puffy white clouds grow taller and darker. Soon, a thunderstorm rolls in.

Thunderstorms begin when warm, wet air rises quickly. As the air climbs, it cools, and its water forms tiny drops and bits of ice. Inside the cloud, these bits bump into each other over and over. The bumping builds up an electric charge, like the static you feel after shuffling across a rug in socks.

When the charge grows strong enough, it jumps as a giant spark. That spark is lightning. A bolt of lightning is hotter than the surface of the sun. It heats the air around it so fast that the air expands with a boom. That boom is thunder.

Because light travels faster than sound, you see the flash before you hear the rumble. You can count the seconds between them to estimate how far away the storm is. About five seconds equals one mile.

Weather experts have a simple rule: when thunder roars, go indoors. A storm may seem far away, but lightning can strike miles from the rain. As the saying goes, it is better to be safe than sorry.$q$, 'draft')
