-- Passage rg5-main-idea-summary-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('3de842a1-0416-5785-979b-fe45deac6f38', 'rg5-main-idea-summary-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Rivers of Ice$q$, $q$A glacier is a huge sheet of ice that moves very slowly over land. Glaciers form in places where more snow falls in winter than melts in summer. Year after year, the snow piles up and presses down. The bottom layers slowly turn into solid ice.

Even though glaciers seem frozen in place, they are always moving. Their great weight pushes them downhill, often just a few feet each day. As a glacier slides, it picks up rocks and soil underneath it.

These rocks work like sandpaper. They scrape and grind the ground as the glacier creeps along. Over thousands of years, a glacier can carve a narrow river valley into a wide valley shaped like the letter U.

When a glacier finally melts, it leaves behind everything it carried. Long ridges of rock and gravel mark where the ice once stopped. Some of the holes it dug fill with water and become lakes. Many lakes in the northern part of North America were formed this way.

Today, scientists study glaciers to learn how the land around us took its shape. The hills, valleys and lakes we see are often clues left by rivers of ice long ago.$q$, 'draft')
