-- Passage rg5-story-details-inference-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d78c8737-9d3c-513a-8983-bea08b7c985b', 'rg5-story-details-inference-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-story-details-inference'), null,
        '3-5', $q$The Rain Gauge$q$, $q$Mateo Reyes taped a ruler to the side of a clear jar and set it on the fence post behind his house. His class was keeping a weather log for two weeks. Each student had to measure the rainfall at home.

On Monday morning the sky was clear, but the jar held almost an inch of water. Mateo frowned. He listened to the weather report on the kitchen radio. It had not rained anywhere in town.

Tuesday was the same. So was Wednesday. Each morning the sky was dry and the jar was not.

On Wednesday night, Mateo set his alarm for five o'clock. He crept out the back door with his jacket over his pajamas and sat on the steps with a flashlight. At a quarter to six, he heard a click and a hiss from the yard next door. A sprinkler rose out of Mr. Okafor's lawn and swung in a slow circle. Every time it turned toward the fence, a spray of water fell into the jar.

Mateo laughed so loudly that a light went on in Mr. Okafor's kitchen.

That afternoon, he carried the jar to the middle of his own yard, far from any fence. In his weather log, he drew a line through three numbers. Beside them he wrote a note: "Not rain. Sprinkler. Ask me for the whole story."$q$, 'draft')
