-- Passage rg5-story-details-inference-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('57f3f8ef-e208-5c35-9fcb-3999632161c3', 'rg5-story-details-inference-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-story-details-inference'), null,
        '3-5', $q$The Weather Vane and the Mole$q$, $q$On top of the old red barn lived an iron rooster, the weather vane. All day it spun on its pole and called down to the animals. "The wind is from the east!" it would cry. "Now it is from the north! I see everything from up here. Ask me anything."

That summer, no rain fell for six weeks. The pond shrank to a muddy puddle, and the animals grew thirsty. They gathered beneath the barn.

"Weather Vane," said the goat, "where can we find water?"

The rooster spun around twice. "The wind is from the south," it announced. "Go south!"

The animals walked south all morning. They found only dry grass and dusty stones. Tired and hot, they trudged back to the barn.

Near the fence, a small mole poked her nose out of the ground. She could barely see the animals at all. "I have not been looking up," she said quietly. "I have been feeling the dirt with my paws. Under the old willow tree, the soil is cool and wet."

The goat dug beneath the willow with his hooves. Soon a little spring of clear water bubbled up.

Above them, the rooster kept spinning. "The wind is from the west!" it called. This time, no one looked up.

Moral: The loudest voice is not always the one that knows the most.$q$, 'draft')
